  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1140 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nAfter Monday, the east coast low will weaken and depart as the\nridge moves in from the west. This will bring a major warmup with\nhighs climbing into the 80s to low 90s by midweek. Low level \nsoutherly flow will develop during the time bringing higher \nhumidity to go along with the building heat.\n\nWe could all use some rain as we wrap up the (likely) warmest and\ndriest September on record for Nashville, but things look dry\nthrough the end of the month. Our National Blend of Models brings\nsome low rain chances starting late Thursday into Friday, then \nincreases those chances over the weekend. Unfortunately, there \ncontinue to be significant differences among the model ensemble \nmembers. The first chances of rain would come as a front \napproaches from the northwest. The better rain chances would\narrive during the weekend as a moisture-rich southern stream \nsystem moves in from the west. Unfortunately, these systems may \nbe slowed or partially deflected by the strong southeast ridge. \nRight now, my personal preference lies with the ECMWF keeping\nthings dry through Friday and much of Saturday based on the strong\nsoutheast ridge. ECMWF then shows a slug of deep moisture and \nsoaking rainfall Saturday night through Sunday. For now, the NBM \nwill spread rain medium chances through the weekend based on the \nuncertainty / varying models. Late week temperatures will be well\nabove normal, but highs could be limited by increasing clouds and\npossible showers and storms.",
